 ## Overview
-Interpolation schemes determine how scalar field values are reconstructed from discrete voxel samples during volume ray casting, directly influencing image quality and computational cost [S1, S2, unofficial]. This family focuses on the spatial reconstruction kernel and weight functions applied to neighboring samples, distinguishing it from sibling techniques like [Multi-Sample Anti-Aliasing via Ray Distribution](1219-multi-sample-anti-aliasing-via-ray-distribution.md) which handles sample distribution, and [Depth to Distance Conversion](2428-depth-to-distance-conversion.md) which handles geometric buffers [G].
+Interpolation schemes for volume rendering reconstruct a continuous scalar field from discrete voxel samples to evaluate density or opacity at arbitrary ray positions [S1, S2, unofficial]. This family solves the problem of discretization artifacts (aliasing, blockiness) inherent in nearest-neighbor sampling while balancing computational cost against spatial smoothness [S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Supersampling](3493-supersampling.md) by defining the *reconstruction kernel* (the shape of the filter) rather than the *sampling pattern* (the number of rays), and from [Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling](826-frequency-domain-volume-rendering-with-sinc-resampling.md) by operating in the spatial domain using fixed local kernels rather than global Fourier transforms [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All interpolation schemes in this family map a query position $\mathbf{p}$ in voxel space to a scalar value $V(\mathbf{p})$ by combining a finite set of neighboring voxel values $V_i$ using a set of weights $w_i(\mathbf{p})$:
 $$ V(\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{i} w_i(\mathbf{p}) V_i $$
-The weights are derived from a reconstruction kernel $h(\mathbf{z})$, where $\mathbf{z}$ is the distance from the query point to the sample center [G]. The choice of kernel defines the trade-off between computational complexity, spatial extent (number of neighbors), and frequency response (aliasing vs. blurring) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The weights are derived from a reconstruction kernel $h(\mathbf{z})$, where $\mathbf{z}$ is the distance from the query point to the sample center [G]. The choice of kernel defines the trade-off between computational complexity, spatial extent (number of neighbors), and frequency response (aliasing vs. blurring) [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
