+### Interpolation Schemes for Projection-Slice Resampling
+Interpolation Schemes for Projection-Slice Resampling are resampling filters applied to extracted projection-slices to control ghosting artifacts and rendering quality during Fourier volume rendering [S7]. Point-sampling (nearest-neighbour) filter clamps missing samples to the nearest available value, causing apparent ghosting artifacts when the volume is rotated [S7]. Trilinear interpolation filter reduces replica intensities and ghosting artifacts compared to point-sampling, with similar performance for slice sizes $\le 512^2$ but higher cost for larger volumes [S7]. Hamming windowed-sinc interpolation filter with width 5 is a high-order reconstruction filter that maximally reduces ghosting artifacts when combined with zero-padding [S7]. Point-sampling and trilinear interpolation have nearly identical performance for slice sizes $\le 512^2$, but trilinear incurs higher performance overhead for larger volumes [S7].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of interpolation in volume rendering emerged with the development of direct volume ray casting in the late 1980s [G]. Levoy's foundational work on ray casting introduced the need to sample continuous volume data, initially relying on simple methods [S2, unofficial]. The distinction between zeroth-order (nearest-neighbor), first-order (linear/trilinear), and higher-order (cubic/sinc) interpolation was formalized to address the trade-off between rendering speed and visual fidelity [S1, S2, unofficial]. Nearest-neighbor interpolation, equivalent to convolving with a rect function (sample-and-hold), was identified as a poor low-pass filter due to passband distortion and stopband leakage, leading to aliasing and blockiness [S2, unofficial]. Linear interpolation suffers from $C^1$ discontinuities (star patterns) [S2, unofficial]. Higher-order methods, such as tri-cubic B-splines and windowed sinc filters (e.g., Blackman window of width four), were developed to eliminate these artifacts for publication-quality imagery, albeit at higher computational cost [S3, S4, unofficial].
+The concept of interpolation in volume rendering originated with the need to reconstruct continuous signals from discrete voxel data. Early work by Westover (1990) introduced footprint evaluation for volume rendering, highlighting the importance of interpolation schemes in determining image quality [S3, S4, unofficial]. Levoy (1988) and Van Hook (1986) established ray-casting methods that relied on linear interpolation for isosurface detection and density estimation [S2, unofficial]. The distinction between nearest-neighbor (zeroth-order), linear (first-order), and cubic (higher-order) interpolation was formalized in early parallel volume rendering algorithms, noting the trade-off between computational cost (1, 8, or 64 samples in 3D) and visual quality [S2, unofficial]. The use of frequency-domain methods, such as the Shear-Warp factorization with sinc resampling, was later developed to address aliasing and blurring artifacts inherent in spatial-domain filters, particularly for large zoom factors [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
